Title: Literature Review — Incremental Sheet Hydroforming & Battery Enclosure (Based only on user‑provided references)

Authors / Compiled by: [Your name]
Date: 2026-09-13

NOTE ON SCOPE
This literature review is strictly limited to the four references supplied by the user (listed at the end). No other sources were consulted. Where a supplied reference does not address a topic (for example, incremental sheet hydroforming), this is explicitly stated and no unsupported claims are made.

1. Executive summary
The supplied references cover lifecycle analysis of lithium‑ion batteries, international abuse testing standards for batteries, and high‑level socio‑economic context for electric vehicles. None of the four references directly address incremental sheet hydroforming (ISH) or detailed battery‑enclosure manufacturing techniques. Therefore, this review extracts enclosure‑relevant findings from the supplied literature and identifies knowledge gaps where ISH‑specific evidence would be required.

2. Columnized literature entries (use Word Insert → Table; columns: Reference | Year | Type | Primary focus | Methods | Key findings | Relevance to battery enclosure / manufacturing)

Reference (authors / citation) | Year | Type | Primary focus | Methods | Key findings | Relevance to battery enclosure / manufacturing
--- | ---: | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---
Dai Q, Kelly JC, Gaines L, Wang M. Life cycle analysis of lithium‑ion batteries for automotive applications. Batteries. 2019;5(2):48. https://doi.org/10.3390/batteries5020048. | 2019 | Peer‑reviewed article (LCA) | Life cycle environmental impacts of lithium‑ion batteries for vehicles | Life cycle assessment (LCA) using inventory data and scenarios | Identifies key life cycle stages (material extraction, cell manufacture, use‑phase, end‑of‑life) that dominate environmental impacts; highlights material choice and manufacturing energy as important drivers | Implication: material selection and manufacturing process for enclosures (including forming methods) affect overall battery system LCA; manufacturing processes with lower energy use or that enable recyclability are preferred. No direct data on ISH present in this reference.
Ruiz V, Pfrang A, Kriston A, Omar N, Van den Bossche P, Boon‑Brett L. A review of international abuse testing standards and regulations for lithium ion batteries in electric and hybrid electric vehicles. Renew Sustain Energy Rev. 2018;81:1427–52. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rser.2017.05.195. | 2018 | Review article (standards) | Survey of international abuse testing standards and regulations for Li‑ion batteries in EVs | Literature and standards review (UL, ISO, SAE, national regs) | Compiles and compares mechanical, thermal, electrical abuse tests required by different jurisdictions; emphasizes the need for robust pack and enclosure design to pass mechanical abuse, thermal runaway, and ingress tests | Directly relevant to enclosure structural requirements: enclosures must satisfy tests for mechanical shock, vibration, penetration, thermal exposure and containment/venting. ISH is not discussed; any manufacturing method (including ISH) used to produce enclosures should meet the standards summarized here.
Faria MV, Baptista PC, Farias TL. Electric vehicle parking in European and American context: Economic, energy and environmental analysis. Transp Res Part A Policy Pract. 2014;64:110–21. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tra.2014.03.011. | 2014 | Research article (policy/analysis) | Economic, energy and environmental analysis of EV parking infrastructure in EU and US | Scenario analysis, energy modeling, policy discussion | Discusses broader EV ecosystem impacts and infrastructure needs; relevant to adoption and deployment economics | Relevance to enclosure design is indirect: market and infrastructure conditions influence cost targets and manufacturing scale for EV components, including battery enclosures. Note: this reference predates 2015 but was supplied by the user and is included per instruction.
Rajper SZ, Albrecht J. Prospects of electric vehicles in the developing countries: a literature review. Sustainability. 2020;12(5):1906. https://doi.org/10.3390/su12051906. | 2020 | Review article (policy/market) | Prospects and barriers for EV adoption in developing countries | Literature review and synthesis of economic, policy, and infrastructure factors | Identifies cost sensitivity, supply chain constraints, and need for low‑cost, reliable EV components for broader adoption | Implication: for EVs in cost‑sensitive markets (including many two‑wheeler markets), enclosure manufacturing must balance cost, durability, and reparability; manufacturing methods that reduce part count or enable low‑cost production are advantageous. No ISH discussion in this reference.

3. Thematic synthesis restricted to supplied references
- Environmental and life‑cycle considerations (Dai et al., 2019): Material selection and manufacturing energy are significant contributors to the environmental footprint of battery systems. While this reference does not evaluate enclosure‑scale manufacturing processes, it implies that enclosure manufacturing choices (material and process) can affect the battery system LCA and should be considered when selecting forming methods.

- Safety and testing requirements (Ruiz et al., 2018): A comprehensive overview of abuse testing standards demonstrates that enclosures must resist mechanical abuse, provide thermal protection/venting, and maintain electrical isolation after prescribed tests. Any enclosure manufacturing technique must support geometries, joining methods, and tolerances that enable meeting these standards.

- Market and cost drivers (Faria et al., 2014; Rajper & Albrecht, 2020): Economic analyses and adoption prospects indicate strong cost sensitivity in certain markets (notably developing countries and two‑wheeler segments). Enclosure manufacturing must therefore target low cost, high reliability, and scalable production.

4. Incremental Sheet Hydroforming (ISH/ISHF): evidence from supplied references
None of the four supplied references mention or discuss incremental sheet hydroforming (ISH or ISHF) or any specific sheet forming technique. Therefore, based solely on these references, no literature‑based conclusions about ISH performance, forming limits, or suitability for battery enclosure manufacture can be drawn.

5. Research and design implications (derived only from supplied references)
- Prioritize safety compliance: Ensure any proposed enclosure design and manufacturing route supports compliance with abuse and safety standards summarized by Ruiz et al. (2018). This includes mechanical integrity under shock/vibration, thermal containment/venting strategies, and electrical isolation post‑abuse.

- Consider life cycle impacts: Use LCA principles (Dai et al., 2019) when selecting materials and manufacturing processes for enclosures—balance weight reduction benefits against manufacturing energy and end‑of‑life recyclability.

- Align with market constraints: For cost‑sensitive markets (Rajper & Albrecht, 2020; Faria et al., 2014), emphasize low‑cost manufacturability, ease of repair, and supply‑chain feasibility when choosing enclosure materials and processes.

- Gap identification and next steps (explicitly outside supplied references): Because ISH is not covered in the supplied literature, targeted studies are required to evaluate ISH for battery enclosures. Recommended studies (to be supported by additional literature and experiments) include:
  - Experimental forming trials of candidate enclosure materials (aluminum alloys, steels, polymers) using ISH to measure thickness distribution, springback, and achievable geometries.
  - Mechanical testing of ISH‑formed enclosures under standardized abuse tests (per Ruiz et al.) to verify compliance.
  - LCA comparing ISH to conventional forming/joining routes for enclosures, following methodologies in Dai et al.
  - Cost and scalability assessment for ISH in low‑cost EV markets, informed by market factors in Rajper & Albrecht and Faria et al.

6. Limitations
This review is strictly limited to the four references provided by the user. Because none of these references address incremental sheet hydroforming, the document does not contain primary-source evidence about ISH; it instead extracts enclosure‑relevant insights from the supplied literature and identifies gaps that would need to be filled by ISH‑specific research.

